## Key decisions

- **WebSocket library: `github.com/coder/websocket`.** Context-aware,
  zero-dependency, net/http-native. Chosen over gorilla/websocket (no
  contexts, chattier API) and gobwas/ws (lower level than this needs).
- **Persistence: SQLite via `modernc.org/sqlite`**, pure Go, no cgo, with
  the single-writer/many-reader pool split — same as the sibling
  kikapcsologo backend, so the tooling and query conventions carry over.
- **Hand-written SQL in `internal/model/`**, no code generation.
- **Fan-out is per-channel, never global.** Each channel owns its member
  set and does its own broadcast; the hub's map is only touched on
  join/part/lookup.
- **Backpressure never propagates into the hub.** A full outbound buffer
  disconnects that client; it must not block the sender.
- **Wire protocol is `VERB {json}` text frames**, one command per frame.
- **Swappable `Broadcaster` interface in `internal/broadcast`.** The fan-out
  is the component most likely to be rewritten, so alternatives are added
  to one table and are measured by the existing tests and benchmarks
  without touching a caller.
- **Fan-out benchmarks count deliveries, not calls.** A drop-on-full
  broadcaster looks fastest when it fails fastest, so every fan-out
  benchmark reports `delivered/sent` and `drops/op` next to `ns/op`.
  Comparisons are made with a paced sender: an implementation whose send
  path is O(1) has nothing pacing it against its receivers and will lap
  them instead of feeding them.
- **Rate limiting is mechanism in the core, policy in a hook.** Token
  buckets live in `internal/ratelimit`; the `Limiter` hook is asked once
  per connection and once per channel for the numbers and never sees a
  message. Unlimited is the default and costs a nil check.
- **Everything that is not moving bytes between sockets is a hook.**
  `internal/hook` defines auth, chatter data, message filtering and
  persistence as interfaces; the core imports the interfaces and never an
  implementation. The zero `Hooks` is a working server.
- **The wire format is negotiated, not configured.** `proto.Codec` is an
  interface over bytes, selected by WebSocket subprotocol. MessagePack
  preferred, JSON the default for anything that did not ask. A ring holds
  encoded bytes, so there is one broadcaster per codec.
- **The write pump takes batches, not single messages.** `Sub.Recv` fills a
  slice, so one wakeup drains everything pending and can coalesce it into a
  single socket write. This is what makes shared-storage fan-out possible
  at all.

